ersion` to understand the API.\n\nLet me also consider: maybe `Version` has `__cmp__` and the `cmp` function... In distutils version.py:\n\n```python\nclass Version:\n    def __init__(self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return \"%s ('%s')\" % (self.__class__.__name__, str(self))\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        ...\n\n    def __cmp__(self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, StringTypes):\n            other = self.__class__(other)\n        ...\n```\n\nActually in distutils, `Version` has `__cmp__` and the class defines `__eq__`, `__ne__`, `__lt__`, etc. via `_cmp`? Let me recall the ansible compat version:\n\nThe ansible module `ansible/module_utils/compat/version.py` is a copy of distutils version.py with modifications. In distutils:\n\n```python\nclass Version:\n\n    def __init__ (self, vstring=None):\n        if vstring:\n            self.parse(vstring)\n\n    def __repr__ (self):\n        return \"%s ('%s')\" % (self.__class__.__name__, str(self))\n\n    def __str__ (self):\n        return self.vstring\n\n    def __cmp__ (self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, StringTypes):\n            other = self.__class__(other)\n\n        if self.version < other.version:\n            return -1\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, but the code shown has `_cmp`. In newer Python distutils (3.10+?), `Version` has:\n\n```python\n    def _cmp(self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, StringTypes):\n            other = self.__class__(other)\n\n        if self.version < other.version:\n            return -1\n        if self.version > other.version:\n            return 1\n        # have to compare prerelease\n        ...\n```\n\nAnd StrictVersion overrides `_cmp` with the prerelease logic. Yes, in Python 3.10+ distutils, `Version._cmp` exists and `StrictVersion._cmp` is the one shown. The operators are defined as:\n\n```python\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        c = self._cmp(other)\n        if c is NotImplemented:\n            return c\n        return c == 0\n\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        ...\n```\n\nSo the ansible compat version.py likely has the same structure. The `StrictVersion._cmp` shown matches distutils' `StrictVersion._cmp`:\n\n```python\n    def _cmp (self, other):\n        if isinstance(other, str):\n            other = StrictVersion(other)\n        elif not isinstance(other, StrictVersion):\n            return NotImplemented\n\n        if self.version != other.version:\n            # numeric versions don't match\n            # prerelease stuff doesn't matter\n            if self.version < other.version:\n                return -1\n            else:\n                return 1\n\n        # have to compare prerelease\n        # case 1: neither has prerelease; they're equal\n        # case 2: self has prerelease, other doesn't; other is greater\n        # case 3: self doesn't have prerelease, other does: self is greater\n        # case 4: both have prerelease: must compare them!\n\n        if (not self.prerelease and not other.prerelease):\n            return 0\n        elif (self.prerelease and not other.prerelease):\n            return -1\n        elif (not self.prerelease and other.prerelease):\n            return 1\n        elif (self.prerelease and other.prerelease):\n            if self.prerelease == other.prerelease:\n                return 0\n            elif self.prerelease < other.prerelease:\n                return -1\n            else:\n                return 1\n        else:\n            raise AssertionError(\"never get here\")\n```\n\nYes, that's exactly distutils' code. Good.\n\nNow, let me use get_info to verify. I'll query `StrictVersion`, `Version`, and maybe `StrictVersion._cmp`.\n\nThe get_info tool: \"Returns information about a symbol.\" with name like 'f' for function f or 'C.foo' for method foo in class C. So I can call `get_info('StrictVersion')`, `get_info('StrictVersion._cmp')`, `get_info('Version')`.\n\nLet me make these